From 3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 15:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: add measured capacity-dmips-mhz
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Set capacity-dmips-mhz on all eight cpu@N so the scheduler sees this SoC
as the asymmetric machine it is: 454 on the six Cortex-A55, 1024 on the
two Cortex-A76.

WHY IT MATTERS. Without the property anywhere, every CPU keeps the
default capacity of 1024 (`cpu_scale`, drivers/base/topology.c).
`asym_cpu_capacity_scan()` then finds a single capacity value and drops
the list again, `asym_cpu_capacity_classify()` returns no flags because
`count < 2`, and `SD_ASYM_CPUCAPACITY` is never set. The kernel runs fine
— it simply treats an A55 and an A76 as interchangeable, so misfit
migration never fires and EAS cannot be enabled at all
(`sched_is_eas_possible()`: "CPUs do not have asymmetric capacities").
The property is also all-or-nothing per the binding: specify it on one
cpu node and you must specify it on all, or every CPU falls back to the
default.

WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM. It is measured on this silicon, not copied.
The binding defines the value as a benchmark score divided by the
frequency it ran at, normalised to the fastest core type — so no
datasheet can supply it, and the A733 UM does not.

Measured on our chain, 2026-07-21, with
a benchmark from the test initramfs — a fixed integer
workload, pinned with sched_setaffinity and confirmed with getcpu(),
timed with CLOCK_MONOTONIC, with a CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID guard that
discards any run that lost the CPU, and a checksum that must be identical
on every core (a differing checksum would mean the cores did not do the
same work, making the ratio meaningless):

  A55 (cpu0)   67 435 180 iters/s   best of 3
  A76 (cpu6)  152 221 250 iters/s   best of 3
  checksum 429933922257178114 identical across all 6 clean runs

Both clusters ran at 1014 MHz, read out of CPU_L_PLL and CPU_B_PLL
(N=39, 26 MHz DCXO) — our kernel has no
cpufreq driver, so the registers are the only source and there is nothing
to divide out. The ratio is therefore already per-MHz:

  152221250 / 67435180 = 2.257   ->   1024 / 2.257 = 453.7  ->  454

CROSS-CHECK AT A DIFFERENT OPERATING POINT. The same binary on the 6 GiB board
(vendor BSP kernel) ran the clusters at 1794 and 2002 MHz: 119 278 792 and
300 599 997 iters/s, i.e. 66 488 and 150 150 per MHz, ratio 2.258 -> 453.
Two boards, two kernels, frequencies differing by a factor of two, and the
normalised result agrees to 0.05 %. That is the invariance the property is
supposed to have, and it is the main reason to trust the figure.

WHAT THIS NUMBER IS NOT. It is not Dhrystone. Dhrystone is dominated by
string and record copies, pointer chasing and enum compares against a
libc, none of which a freestanding binary has. What was measured is a
mixed integer workload — multiply, xorshift ALU, rotate-and-fold, an
L1-resident load-modify-store and a predictable branch — chosen so that
independent work is available for a wide out-of-order core to find, since
that ability is exactly what separates an A76 from an in-order A55. A
different op-mix would move the ratio; there is no neutral mix. So this is
"our measured mixed-ALU ratio", and it should be read that way rather than
as a DMIPS claim.

FOR COMPARISON, deliberately not adopted: the Allwinner BSP ships 430 for
the A55 (a value that appears nowhere in mainline), and the in-flight
mainline A733 dtsi carries 530, which is the rk3588 figure for the same
core pair. Mainline A55 values range from 250 to 1024 across vendors and
A76 from 620 to 1740, so there is no convention to follow — only vendor
measurements of varying provenance. 454 sits between the two candidates
and is the only one that was measured on this SoC.

NOT INCLUDED: dynamic-power-coefficient. It is an absolute quantity
(uW/MHz/V^2) rather than a ratio, it needs OPP voltages to be useful, and
we have neither the power measurements nor a cpufreq driver. Without it
the Energy Model is not registered and EAS stays off even with asymmetric
capacities, so this patch improves misfit handling and capacity-aware
placement, not EAS.

Depends on `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-topology`. DT-only.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
